The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
ret = i2c_smbus_read_block_data(client, PMBUS_IC_DEVICE_ID, buf);
if (ret < 0)
    return ret;
buf_len = (buf[ret - 1] != '\x00' ? ret : ret - 1);
```
Reason: When `i2c_smbus_read_block_data()` returns 0 (zero-length read), the condition `ret < 0` is false, so execution continues. Then `buf[ret - 1]` becomes `buf[-1]`, which is an out-of-bounds access on the stack.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
ret = i2c_smbus_read_block_data(client, PMBUS_IC_DEVICE_ID, buf);
if (ret <= 0)
    return ret < 0 ? ret : -EIO;
buf_len = (buf[ret - 1] != '\x00' ? ret : ret - 1);
```
Reason: Changing the condition to `ret <= 0` treats both negative returns (error) and zero-length read (`ret == 0`) as errors. For `ret == 0`, we return `-EIO`, which prevents the out-of-bounds array access. For `ret < 0`, the original error code is still returned. The rest of the code now only executes when `ret > 0`, ensuring safe array access.

